  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「ヨルガオ」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%83%A8%E3%83%AB%E3%82%AC%E3%82%AA",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 109360706,
      "text": "ヨルガオ は、ヒルガオ科サツマイモ属の植物の一種。ヤカイソウ（夜会草）とも呼ぶ"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Ipomoea alba」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ipomoea_alba",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1371934383,
      "text": "Ipomoea alba, known in English as  white tropical morning glory, evening glory, moonflower or moonvine, is a species of night-blooming morning glory, native to tropical and subtropical regions of North and South America, from Argentina to northern Mexico, Arizona, Florida and the West Indies. Though formerly classified as genus Calonyction, species aculeatum, it is now properly assigned to genus Ipomoea, subgenus Quamoclit, section Calonyction."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "cultivation": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Ipomoea alba」の節「Cultivation」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ipomoea_alba#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 1371934383,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "The species is widely cultivated as an ornamental plant for its flowers. In areas too cold for winter survival, it can be grown as an annual plant. Since it is of tropical origin, it flowers best under a summer short-day photoperiod. Though it can be successfully flowered in the north, its flowering is impaired by excessively long summer days. Thus, at higher latitudes it often does not set buds and bloom until early autumn, when daylight length is once again near 12 hours.\nPropagation is usually by seed. The seed resembles a small, brownish nut, and should be nicked with a file and then soaked overnight before planting.\nIn some areas, the plant is an invasive species which can cause problems in agricultural settings.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
  },
